## Explicit exclusions

- `.git/` is repository metadata, so agents do not treat its internals as product context.
- `.worktrees/` and harness-managed worktree directories are execution infrastructure, so they are not product areas.
- `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are third-party dependency caches, so their generated contents are not repository knowledge.
- `dist/`, `build/`, `coverage/`, and `.cache/` are generated outputs or caches, so their source directories remain the authority.
- `plugins/bizcity-facebook-bot/logs/`, `error_log`, and other runtime logs are operational output, so they are not context sources.
- `tests/_archived/` is historical test material, so current test behavior is owned by the active test directories.

An unlisted directory is a gap in this file, not an ignorable area.

## Universal STOP rules

- STOP before publicly disclosing a suspected vulnerability because coordinated disclosure protects users; follow `SECURITY.md` for the full reporting policy.
- STOP before bypassing a framework ownership or public-contract boundary because parallel implementations cause drift; follow `docs/framework/FRAMEWORK-GUIDE-v1.md` for the full workflow.
- STOP before implementing server-only `bizcity-llm-router` behavior here because this is the WordPress client repository; follow `CONTRIBUTING.md` for the repository boundary and contribution rules.

## Architecture and decision records

- Pipeline orchestration architecture: `core/intent/docs/PIPELINE-ARCHITECTURE.md` is the single home for the pipeline plan schema, executor design, lifecycle, integration, async support, and rollout record.

## Enforcement pointers

Automated PHP style enforcement is configured in `phpcs.xml.dist`, and repository CI entry points live in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`; those files remain the authority for machine-enforced checks.
